  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $75$, and the common ratio is $5.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,713?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=75$ and  $r=5$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{3-1} \\\\ 75 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\ 75 & =25{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {5}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,713.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {5}^{n-1} & >1\\,713 \\\\ {5}^{n-1} & >571 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}\u2061{5}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}\u2061(571) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}\u2061(5) & >\\operatorname{log}\u2061(571) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(571)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(5)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(571)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(5)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(571)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(5)}+1\u22484.944\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $5$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,713.$",
